<title>UK: UK May PSBR (budget deficit) 3.96 bln stg.</title>
<headline>UK May PSBR (budget deficit) 3.96 bln stg.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>UK public sector borrowing requirement data for May. Deficits unless noted.</p>
<p> PSBR (in million stg)		  MAY     APR-MAY    APR-MAY</p>
<p>						    1997/98    1996/97</p>
<p> Public sector			3,955    4,031	6,168</p>
<p> Central government		 5,404    3,937	4,458</p>
<p> Central government own acct    4,114    3,060	5,640</p>
<p> Local Authority			 46REP 1,207	1,299</p>
<p>  from central govt		 1,225	740	   57</p>
<p> General Government		 4,133    4,404	5,700</p>
<p> Public Corporation		   113REP   236REP     771REP</p>
<p>  from central govt		    65	137	1,239 REP</p>
<p>GOVERNMENT RECEIPTS</p>
<p> Central govt total receipts   18,394   42,094     40,265</p>
<p> Inland Revenue receipts	  5,450   14,970     13,893</p>
<p>  of which income tax	     4,941   11,409     10,599</p>
<p>  corporation tax		     183    2,420	2,531</p>
<p> Customs and excise		 7,029   14,442     13,620</p>
<p>  of which VAT			4,265    8,981	8,144</p>
<p> Social security		    3,903    8,602	7,932</p>
<p> Interest				 484    1,161	1,242</p>
<p> Other				  1,528    2,919	3,578</p>
<p>CENTRAL GOVERNMENT OUTLAYS</p>
<p>  Total				22,508   45,154     45,905</p>
<p>   of which Interest		1,793    3,477	3,754</p>
<p>   Net departmental outlays    20,936   42,070     43,352</p>
<p>NOTE- Privatisation proceeds in May were 221 million stg, compared with 1,123 million in May last year.</p>
<p>Consensus forecast was for a 3.0 bln stg public sector deficit in May.</p>
<p>April PSBR was revised to 76 million stg from the originally reported 36 million stg.  </p>
<p>The ONS said privatisation proceeds were due to early receipts from the second call on Railtrack shares.</p>
<p>It said June PSBR would include privatisation proceeds from the second call on Railtrack shares of 700 million stg.</p>
<p>VAT receipts in June are expected to be lower than in the same period last year while June is increasingly a peak month for interest payments because most gilts are now being issued with aligned coupon dates in June and September.</p>
</text>
